Key it by `req.client.client_id`, not IP — the auth middleware in `routes/api.js` already resolves that from the API key, and the code's own comment notes clients share NAT'd addresses, so IP would throttle the wrong people.

State has to live in Redis, not process memory: `app.js` forks 4 workers per box behind a load balancer, so any in-process counter only sees a quarter of one box's traffic. `redis.js` already gives you a shared client — reuse it.

Add a second middleware in `routes/api.js`, after the auth lookup (so `req.client` is populated) and before the route handlers:

```js
const WINDOW_S = 60;
const LIMITS = { free: 60, pro: 600, enterprise: 3000 }; // req/min

router.use(async (req, res, next) => {
  const limit = LIMITS[req.client.plan] ?? LIMITS.free;
  const key = `rl:${req.client.client_id}:${Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000 / WINDOW_S)}`;
  const count = await redis.client.incr(key);
  if (count === 1) await redis.client.expire(key, WINDOW_S);
  if (count > limit) {
    res.set("Retry-After", String(WINDOW_S));
    return res.status(429).end();
  }
  next();
});
```

Fixed-window `INCR`+`EXPIRE` is two round trips but atomic enough for this (no read-modify-write race); a sliding-window log or token bucket is the next level down if you need smoother burst handling.

